WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:02.802
-Welcome back to "Late Night."
We are here with Ana Gasteyer.

00:00:02.802 --> 00:00:04.571
We were talking
about the holidays.

00:00:04.571 --> 00:00:07.206
You have a daughter in college.
You have a son in eighth grade.

00:00:07.206 --> 00:00:09.241
And I heard you gave
your eighth-grader

00:00:09.241 --> 00:00:11.243
a very beloved gift.

00:00:11.243 --> 00:00:14.480
-Well, so, I was locked in
the room because I had COVID.

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:16.616
And so my plan was,
as it often is --

00:00:16.616 --> 00:00:20.053
'cause it was a panicky year.
I had a very busy fall.

00:00:20.053 --> 00:00:23.055
So my plan is, usually,
to land in Albuquerque,

00:00:23.055 --> 00:00:25.458
where my folks live, and then
I hit Target, hit Costco,

00:00:25.458 --> 00:00:27.794
you know, do a bunch
of last-minute shopping.

00:00:27.794 --> 00:00:29.963
But because I got sick,
I was screwed.

00:00:29.963 --> 00:00:32.498
I had like four gifts
total planned,

00:00:32.498 --> 00:00:34.634
and most of them
were not here in New York.

00:00:34.634 --> 00:00:35.869
So I panicked.

00:00:35.869 --> 00:00:38.971
I turned to the hive-mind
of the "SNL" ladies.

00:00:38.971 --> 00:00:41.040
Our friend Paula Pell naturally
came up with a gift --

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:42.208
best gift of all time,

00:00:42.208 --> 00:00:44.977
which is a remote-controlled
fart machine.

00:00:44.977 --> 00:00:46.612
And I really can't
recommend this more.

00:00:46.612 --> 00:00:49.682
It's a crowd-pleaser not just
for my 13-year-old son,

00:00:49.682 --> 00:00:51.417
but for people everywhere --
for people and friends.

00:00:51.417 --> 00:00:52.819
And I got Amazon's choice,

00:00:52.819 --> 00:00:54.853
so I got the highest quality one
that I could.

00:00:54.853 --> 00:00:57.723
You know, I went for the --
I did my research.

00:00:57.723 --> 00:00:59.525
You know, I'm not gonna be
leaving a nasty review there.

00:00:59.525 --> 00:01:00.926
And I gotta tell you something.

00:01:00.926 --> 00:01:04.530
This gift was such
a four-star -- five-star --

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:07.500
five-star -- four out of four,
five out of five, winner.

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:10.035
So if you are looking for,
you know,

00:01:10.035 --> 00:01:13.039
a last-minute Valentine's gift
or whatever for your wife,

00:01:13.039 --> 00:01:16.209
it is something that
can really fill in in a pinch.

00:01:16.209 --> 00:01:17.877
And we've been using it
around the home.

00:01:17.877 --> 00:01:19.512
It just comes up.
It comes up all the time.

00:01:19.512 --> 00:01:21.114
It's very useful.

00:01:21.114 --> 00:01:24.817
-And does it play into the video
that you brought for us?

00:01:24.817 --> 00:01:27.487
-Oh, yeah, this is the ways
in which it can be used

00:01:27.487 --> 00:01:29.288
to humiliate your friends
and relatives.

00:01:29.288 --> 00:01:31.558
I mean, that's the function of a
remote-controlled fart machine,

00:01:31.558 --> 00:01:33.592
'cause you can plant it,
walk away, and then --

00:01:33.592 --> 00:01:36.629
It's a very spot-on and very --

00:01:36.629 --> 00:01:39.465
The library of fart sounds
I find very well-curated.

00:01:39.465 --> 00:01:41.835
It is not your garden-variety
fart machine.

00:01:41.835 --> 00:01:44.237
They've really gone
the extra mile.

00:01:44.237 --> 00:01:47.940
But I did hear some chuckles.

00:01:47.940 --> 00:01:49.542
It's a self-amusement toy.

00:01:49.542 --> 00:01:51.344
I walked through because
we had the ice skating on --

00:01:51.344 --> 00:01:52.711
because we've gotta know
what's coming up

00:01:52.711 --> 00:01:54.614
for the United States Olympics.

00:01:54.614 --> 00:01:55.948
-Right.
-Olympic team.

00:01:55.948 --> 00:01:58.584
And so I walked through
my living room the other day

00:01:58.584 --> 00:02:01.687
to find an incredibly
self-satisfied 13-year-old,

00:02:01.687 --> 00:02:03.122
and that's what the video is.

00:02:03.122 --> 00:02:05.658
-The next thought I had -- I was
just jotting down this note --

00:02:05.658 --> 00:02:07.093
is I --
[ Farting ]

00:02:07.093 --> 00:02:08.962
...program this music on him
so much.

00:02:08.962 --> 00:02:11.464
It matches his personality.
Very debonair.

00:02:11.464 --> 00:02:17.236
♪♪

00:02:17.236 --> 00:02:19.172
[ Fart ]

00:02:19.172 --> 00:02:21.808
[ Farting ]

00:02:22.708 --> 00:02:24.377
-You know, I really tip my cap.

00:02:24.377 --> 00:02:26.312
A very judicious use of it.

00:02:26.312 --> 00:02:27.680
Didn't overdo it.

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:29.681
Perfectly landed each one.

00:02:29.681 --> 00:02:32.351
-Yeah, well, you've gotta
really take your time with it.

00:02:32.351 --> 00:02:35.120
Exactly. And you never know
what's coming down the pipe.

00:02:35.120 --> 00:02:36.189
Thank you, thank you.

00:02:36.189 --> 00:02:37.857
I'll let him know
that you approved.

00:02:37.857 --> 00:02:40.092
That was Ulysses McKitrick's
craftsmanship.

00:02:40.092 --> 00:02:41.961
I think we've got a future
as a sound editor.

00:02:41.961 --> 00:02:43.829
-Yes, let's make sure
he gets his credit,

00:02:43.829 --> 00:02:45.632
because the credit is due.

00:02:45.632 --> 00:02:49.135
So, we overlapped for a year
at "SNL," Ana.

00:02:49.135 --> 00:02:52.171
And then we got to work together
one more time

00:02:52.171 --> 00:02:55.374
on the very famous
Betty White episode.

00:02:55.374 --> 00:02:58.144
Betty sadly passed,
but it was so nice

00:02:58.144 --> 00:03:02.515
to be able to share with people
how special that week was.

00:03:02.515 --> 00:03:05.718
And you were a part of,
you know, for my money,

00:03:05.718 --> 00:03:08.288
some of the best moments
of that episode.

00:03:08.288 --> 00:03:10.455
What do you remember
from that week?

00:03:10.455 --> 00:03:11.958
-That was a magical week.

00:03:11.958 --> 00:03:13.326
I know we've talked
about it before.

00:03:13.326 --> 00:03:16.662
I actually was so touched
that NBC re-aired it.

00:03:16.662 --> 00:03:19.065
I thought it was such
a great way to honor her,

00:03:19.065 --> 00:03:21.601
because I always say
show business is the
fountain of youth,

00:03:21.601 --> 00:03:24.370
that people who live in our
world have this kind of --

00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:25.537
You have to be so elastic,

00:03:25.537 --> 00:03:26.672
and you have to
keep up with the times.

00:03:26.672 --> 00:03:28.107
And she was such, as you know,

00:03:28.107 --> 00:03:30.676
a perfect, perfect
living example of that.

00:03:30.676 --> 00:03:33.645
She hosted when she was 88,
and she may as well have been --

00:03:33.645 --> 00:03:34.947
whatever -- 25.

00:03:34.947 --> 00:03:37.115
I mean, she was so up
on every joke, every nuance.

00:03:37.115 --> 00:03:39.452
And looking back on the show --
I hadn't watched it

00:03:39.452 --> 00:03:43.856
since we did it 11 years ago
until last Saturday.

00:03:43.856 --> 00:03:45.692
Everything's just so --

00:03:45.692 --> 00:03:47.994
You know, there's some racy
stuff in there, and she was --

00:03:47.994 --> 00:03:50.229
she understood
every single joke.

00:03:50.229 --> 00:03:51.997
She knew how to set it up,
how to walk away.

00:03:51.997 --> 00:03:53.866
You know what I mean?
-Yeah.

00:03:53.866 --> 00:03:55.367
-Yeah, so, for me, that week,

00:03:55.367 --> 00:03:58.570
it definitely was
the beginning of --

00:03:58.570 --> 00:04:00.205
Not to be all artsy-fartsy

00:04:00.205 --> 00:04:01.774
or, you know, whatever,
psych about it.

00:04:01.774 --> 00:04:04.811
It was a really cool week
because it brought together

00:04:04.811 --> 00:04:08.580
a lot of women from sort of
two generations of "SNL"

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:10.683
and it helped kind of
solidify this idea

00:04:10.683 --> 00:04:12.718
of a female presence
on the show, I think.

00:04:12.718 --> 00:04:14.820
It was by accident
because it was Mother's Day

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:16.522
and we were really there
for her.

00:04:16.522 --> 00:04:20.760
But I think our shared adulation
of her and the community

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:23.196
and writing together with other
women, it was pretty incredible.

00:04:23.196 --> 00:04:27.500
It set in motion
a real sorority, as you know,

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:29.802
that exists amongst us, so...

00:04:29.802 --> 00:04:31.503
-Well, it's great because,
you know,

00:04:31.503 --> 00:04:33.906
not just films
like "Wine Country"

00:04:33.906 --> 00:04:35.407
that happened after the fact

00:04:35.407 --> 00:04:37.476
that, you know,
collects everybody

00:04:37.476 --> 00:04:39.412
who came back for that show,
but also --

00:04:39.412 --> 00:04:40.913
You know,
we've talked about it before,

00:04:40.913 --> 00:04:42.681
and I've talked about it
with pretty much everybody

00:04:42.681 --> 00:04:44.617
on said text chain,

00:04:44.617 --> 00:04:49.087
but you guys are on the hottest
text chain in the biz,

00:04:49.087 --> 00:04:54.760
which is these
"SNL" female, you know, alumni.

00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:57.329
And I'm deeply jealous
not to be a part of it,

00:04:57.329 --> 00:05:00.098
but I'm just happier
knowing that it exists.

00:05:00.098 --> 00:05:01.500
-It definitely exists.

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:02.801
It's a huge part
of all of our lives.

00:05:02.801 --> 00:05:04.269
And again, like I've said,

00:05:04.269 --> 00:05:07.039
we've talked about it,
this sorority of girlfriends.

00:05:07.039 --> 00:05:08.908
I mean, mostly, at this point,
it's just, you know,

00:05:08.908 --> 00:05:12.245
recipes and, you know,
Christmas gift ideas

00:05:12.245 --> 00:05:13.813
such as remote-control
fart machines.

00:05:13.813 --> 00:05:15.981
But, you know,
a fountain of [Laughs]

00:05:15.981 --> 00:05:18.584
a fountain of useful
information, to be honest.

00:05:18.584 --> 00:05:20.819
But, yeah, I think
the Betty part of it

00:05:20.819 --> 00:05:23.822
is that she really was
the cornerstone,

00:05:23.822 --> 00:05:25.191
which is really incredible

00:05:25.191 --> 00:05:28.060
when I think about what was
set in motion, you know?

00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:31.130
-Yeah, it's really amazing
that, again,

00:05:31.130 --> 00:05:34.166
I mean, not just that she could
have that incredible episode.

00:05:34.166 --> 00:05:36.636
And then for the last 10 years
after that episode,

00:05:36.636 --> 00:05:38.937
she did so many other
deeply funny things.

00:05:38.937 --> 00:05:40.873
But she did
bring people together,

00:05:40.873 --> 00:05:44.209
mostly because there was
such a core sweetness to her

00:05:44.209 --> 00:05:46.945
and really reminded everybody
who was there that week,

00:05:46.945 --> 00:05:49.415
"Oh, we should count ourselves
as deeply lucky

00:05:49.415 --> 00:05:51.184
that we get to do this
for a living."

00:05:51.184 --> 00:05:53.352
-Absolutely.
Super optimistic.

00:05:53.352 --> 00:05:55.454
Came from a really
optimistic standpoint.

00:05:55.454 --> 00:05:56.855
Looked for the joke.
Looked for --

00:05:56.855 --> 00:05:58.958
I mean, you know, I remember
everyone was running around

00:05:58.958 --> 00:06:00.660
all nervous that she
was gonna be overtired

00:06:00.660 --> 00:06:02.661
'cause she was doing "MacGruber"
till 3:00 in the morning

00:06:02.661 --> 00:06:03.696
or whatever, you know?
-Yeah.

00:06:03.696 --> 00:06:06.165
-And I remember, like,
saying to her --

00:06:06.165 --> 00:06:07.633
it might have been Saturday
morning, Friday morning --

00:06:07.633 --> 00:06:08.801
"How are you doing?
You worked really late."

00:06:08.801 --> 00:06:10.169
She was like,
"Oh, I went back to my room.

00:06:10.169 --> 00:06:12.171
It was fine. I had a vodka
and a hot dog, and I slept."

00:06:12.171 --> 00:06:15.474
You know, it's sort of like
how you want to live

00:06:15.474 --> 00:06:16.909
at 88, you know?

00:06:16.909 --> 00:06:20.112
Have an vodka -- an vodka --
and an hot dog and go to bed,

00:06:20.112 --> 00:06:24.016
you know, and then wake up and
go do an incredible comedy show

00:06:24.016 --> 00:06:26.519
with a huge number of amazing

00:06:26.519 --> 00:06:29.021
and, you know,
relevant young people.

00:06:29.021 --> 00:06:31.423
Super, super inspiring.

00:06:31.423 --> 00:06:33.525
-Well, it was a special week
to be a part of,

00:06:33.525 --> 00:06:34.727
and it's always great
to see you.

00:06:34.727 --> 00:06:35.828
Thanks so much
for being here, Ana.

00:06:35.828 --> 00:06:37.229
-Hey, thanks for having me.

00:06:37.229 --> 00:06:39.931
Enjoy "American Auto."
I'm really proud of it.

00:06:39.931 --> 00:06:41.166
-Ana Gasteyer, everybody.

00:06:41.166 --> 00:06:43.736
"American Auto" airs
Tuesday nights here on NBC

00:06:43.736 --> 00:06:45.405
and streams any time on Peacock.

